# The Hierarchy of Foreignness · utlanning to varelse

The Hierarchy of Foreignness is the framework Valentine wrote under the name Demosthenes for ranking the Other. It names five degrees of foreignness, ordered by how far apart two beings stand and whether any bridge of understanding remains between them. The utlanning is a stranger of one's own world; the framling, a human of another world; the raman, a being of another species with whom understanding and dialogue are possible — a person; the varelse, a true alien with whom no common ground or communication can be found; and the djur, the monster.

The ladder's hinge is the distinction between raman and varelse. A raman is foreign in body and origin yet reachable in mind — someone with whom dialogue is possible, and therefore a person. A varelse is the alien with whom no meaning can ever be exchanged, no common ground discovered. The whole moral weight of the framework rests on which of these two names we choose to apply, because the name we give decides whether we speak or whether we destroy.

This is the moral spine of the saga: most 'aliens' are raman if only we try, and war comes from mistaking raman for varelse. The Hierarchy reframes conflict between peoples not as inevitable difference but as a failure of attempt — the tragedy of having named a person a monster before the work of understanding was done. To rank the Other rightly is to hold open the possibility of dialogue until it is truly exhausted, rather than presume its absence.

Its emergence is ethereal because the Hierarchy is a connective architecture — it maps the threads of understanding that can bind one kind of being to another, the unseen lattice along which strangers become persons. It does not describe bodies or worldly power but the relational distance between minds, and so belongs to the saga's order of connection rather than to its embodied, natural, or machine domains.
